WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
Film Courage: You had a Tweet recently about action
lines and it got a lot of traction (a lot

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:10.400
of people engaged). Can we start with what
action lines are aimed to accomplish? David Wappel, Screenwriter: I

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:18.119
think that action lines are aimed to
accomplish two things tell you what is

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:30.900
happening but also how we see what is
happening and you can use words to sort

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:37.649
of secretly cue your reader into how
they should be seeing what's happening I

00:00:37.649 --> 00:00:42.719
think one of the coolest parts of
screenwriting is that we can use words

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:49.079
to cue in the readers brain how they see
what's happening even without telling

00:00:49.079 --> 00:00:53.789
them how they should see what's
happening see what kind of an example so

00:00:53.789 --> 00:01:02.940
if we were to say she entered the room
with a sarcastic grin or she crept in

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:11.760
from this I don't know I'm help me out
yeah I think you can if you want to

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.710
start a line a toothy grin spreads
across her face as the doorknob clicks

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:22.770
and she enters the room the first thing
I'm gonna see is that toothy grin so

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:29.100
even though I didn't say close-up on
mouth the reader will probably just see

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:35.790
a mouth grinning because I haven't told
them yet that this is a room so they're

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:40.229
only thinking in terms of what they've
been told because that's just the way

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:43.320
our brain works I'm not gonna imagine
something that no one has queued me to

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:53.579
imagine but if I wanted to say the room
sits vacant as a door opens and she

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:57.750
enters smiling you're already in the
room and you've I've told the reader

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.229
that there's a room which a room is
probably going to cue a wide shot and

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:07.890
then the door opens and she enters and
she has the grin now it's the same

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:13.200
action in both but I have used different
words to hopefully cue the

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.220
into how I think they should be seeing
it and I haven't told the director

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.370
necessarily how to shoot it but I've
suggested what I think might be best for

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.560
the story and then if a director takes
that and says that's great that gives me

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.850
an idea for something even stronger and
they use a wide shot to get what I was

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.900
trying to achieve even better because
I'm not telling them how to do their job

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:40.080
I'm just suggesting it but either way
the director the actress the production

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.980
designer everyone knows that all we need
to do is just get this actress entering

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.780
the room smiling I've just suggested
what I think is the best way to visually

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.220
tell that but even if none of that gets
across she's still entering the room

00:02:53.220 --> 00:03:01.110
smiling and that's as far as I need to
take it I think so the one where she's

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:05.519
smiling or the toothy grin which one do
you think is but I like the toothy grin

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:08.580
in some sense because that keeps me like
hmm

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:14.790
toothy as in showing the canines is it
like a little more mischievous or is it

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:19.350
she's overjoyed I think it would depend
on the context and that those are the

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.310
kind of choices that you get to make as
a screenwriter when you're decided on

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:31.620
about how to tell the audience how to
see what is in your screenplay so if

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:37.459
it's really important to me that is an
establishing shot of this character and

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:43.829
I want the first thing the audience to
see when they first meet his character

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:50.459
is her toothy grin then I'm going to
want to cue that with those words but if

00:03:50.459 --> 00:03:54.480
this is not necessarily that important
or there's other considerations or other

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:58.560
reasons that that's I don't want to drop
too much attention to this character and

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.269
her grin I might not do that and I might
just have her enter the room from the

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:06.600
wide because it's more important that
the room is vacant that's the creative

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:14.609
choice that I want to have in the
screenplay perhaps this is an apartment

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:19.859
that this woman is entering where she
lived with her husband and she's had a

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:23.010
good days so she enters smiling but we
already know it's vacant and we see her

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:25.600
face drop so it's more important that we
see

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.970
apartment vacant first but I could also
flip that we could be on her with the

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:35.380
toothy grin
maybe I just a big smile but I can say

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:40.900
big smile she opens the door and finds
an empty apartment her face falls so I

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.220
can basically even though it's the same
action I can swap those two visually

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:50.560
with my words and actually create
different effects in the audience in one

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:55.450
of your tweets you mentioned writing
action lines with special attention paid

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:02.110
to word order and what you call
anchoring now mm-hmm what is an

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:09.790
anchoring noun and anchoring noun is a
term that I made up because I didn't

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:14.130
know what else to call it but for me
it's the first noun that you encounter

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:21.430
that communicates what sort of shot you
should be seeing the action I'm

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:28.300
describing so if I say an orchard
sprawls across the valley

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:33.790
I haven't said wide shot but orchard to
me is the anchoring noun that anchors

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:37.690
you visually into a wide shot because
you don't picture an orchard and

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:41.440
close-up because I've told you it's an
orchard it's a big thing if I say an

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:47.920
apple sits on a branch then the
anchoring noun would be Apple and I've

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.960
kind of cued you into a closer shot even
though I haven't said camera close-up on

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:59.640
or anything like that so those nouns and
where you place them are just what I

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.380
call anchoring downs because it anchors
you to the image that I want you to

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:10.960
picture in your head and does that also
kind of correspond with grounded stories

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:20.860
grounded cinema in some sense because
it's sort of I mean maybe there's no

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.850
correlation on I'm not sure if it's all
right well because to me this is like a

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:30.210
bit like a it's just like a little crass
thing and depending on people use it

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:36.390
yeah it's uh it's a tool in the toolkit
and I think people can employ it to any

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:45.730
level of stories it can be an incredibly
heightened not grounded like fun space

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:51.510
romp but you can still use anchor nouns
for heightening some of that craziness

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:59.860
what is visual writing in a screenplay
to me visual writing is writing with the

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:08.490
notion that this is something to be seen
so using words that in my mind are

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:16.960
visually sticky so a character walking
across the room doesn't evoke a very

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:22.120
strong visual thing for me but a
character leaping across the room I

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:27.520
guess that seems more visual to me
because it's just more of a specific

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:33.570
action so I can picture a leap that's
very specific and it's different than a

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:38.140
walk okay yeah this walk sort of falls
flat

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:42.940
but with leaping there's more of an
urgency to it whether it's a positive

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:47.800
thing or negative thing mm-hmm okay I
don't know if there's more of an urgency

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:53.530
to it but there's a specificity to it a
character that shuffles across the room

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:58.570
I can picture that and I can picture
that very differently than a leap but a

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:02.140
walk kind of falls flat because we just
see people walk every day so there's

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:08.500
nothing inherently unique about that
action and if the character is just

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:12.400
walking and there's they should not be
doing anything else then I think use

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.330
walk it's not that you have to use
something else but if there's anything

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:23.320
you can give me that is just a little
more specific than walk I'm gonna be

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.280
able to picture it I think a little bit
easier so if you don't want to call

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.800
attention to the walk if it's not really
a major thing in the story then just

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.000
maybe keep it something like that walk
something more bland but if there's a

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:40.180
point to what you're trying to make or
this character is going to be doing

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:43.930
something or setting it up for something
then that's where the more the more

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:50.980
visual the better yeah I think
the more specific the better every noun

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:56.679
and verb in your script is an
opportunity to tell the audience

00:08:56.679 --> 00:09:01.899
something and the more specific you can
be with your nouns and verbs the less

00:09:01.899 --> 00:09:09.790
you have to use adjectives and adverbs
which saves you space and so a character

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:16.420
reluctantly walks is more words than
saying a character shuffles across the

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:21.610
room or a character pads across the room
oh I like where this is going this is

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:27.929
great yeah the more specific you can be
with your nouns and your verbs the less

00:09:27.929 --> 00:09:33.009
adjectives and adverbs you'll have to
use in your screenplay do you see that a

00:09:33.009 --> 00:09:38.110
lot with newer writers I think with
newer writers the thing that I see the

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:46.779
most is more words rather than less so
over written

00:09:46.779 --> 00:09:52.919
I think over written might be a way to
put it

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.370
you

